---
import MainLayout from "../../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
import Container from "../../../components/ui/Container.astro";
import Card from "../../../components/ui/Card.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/ui/CodeBlock.astro";
import Icon from "../../../components/Icon.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from "../../../utils/constants";

const DOCS = "https://jvde-github.github.io/AIS-catcher-docs";
const STORE = "https://www.rtl-sdr.com/buy-rtl-sdr-dvb-t-dongles/";
const installLinux =
  'sudo bash -c "$(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jvde-github/AIS-catcher/main/scripts/aiscatcher-install) -p -M"';
const installMac = [
  "brew install git make gcc cmake pkg-config sqlite3 librtlsdr openssl@3",
  "git clone https://github.com/jvde-github/AIS-catcher.git --depth 1",
  "cd AIS-catcher",
  "mkdir build",
  "cd build",
  'cmake .. -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH="$(brew --prefix openssl@3)"',
  "make",
  "sudo make install",
].join("\n");
const sharingUrl = "wssmqtt://x:YOUR_TOKEN@ais.openwaters.io:443/v1/stream";
const troubleshooting = [
  {
    symptom: "The receiver does not appear",
    action:
      "Check the USB connection and adapter. Close other radio software: only one program can use the dongle at a time. On Windows, check that WinUSB was installed for the RTL-SDR device.",
  },
  {
    symptom: "The receiver appears, but no ships arrive",
    action:
      "Check the antenna cable, both long elements, and their vertical orientation. Try a higher window facing the water, away from your computer and other electronics. Hills, buildings, coated window glass, or a quiet shipping lane can limit reception.",
  },
  {
    symptom: "Local ships appear, but your Open Waters station is quiet",
    action:
      "Check your internet connection and the output URL. Make sure you replaced YOUR_TOKEN, chose NMEA as the message format, enabled the output, then saved and restarted the receiver.",
  },
  {
    symptom: "Reception stops when your computer sleeps",
    action:
      "Keep the computer awake and AIS-catcher running while you want to receive. Start it again after restarting your computer. A dedicated Raspberry Pi is an optional way to keep sharing.",
  },
];
---

<MainLayout
  title="Hear the ships outside your window"
  description="Start receiving AIS from shore with one antenna and USB radio kit and your own computer. See nearby ships, share with Open Waters AIS, then optionally make it permanent."
  image="/og/ais.png"
>
  <Container class="py-12 lg:py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      <a href="/ais/" class="mb-6 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm">
        <Icon name="mdi:arrow-left" class="size-4" /> Open Waters AIS
      </a>
      <h1 class="mb-6">Hear the ships outside your window.</h1>
      <p class="text-lead mb-6">
        Ships broadcast their name and position by radio. An antenna and a small
        USB receiver let your computer hear nearby ships and share them with
        Open Waters AIS.
      </p>
      <p class="text-fg-muted mb-8">
        Start at home, with the computer you already own. You don't need a boat
        or a permanent outdoor installation. Already using Signal K aboard? See <a
          href="/ais/signalk/">AIS for Signal K</a
        >.
      </p>
      <nav aria-label="Guide sections" class="mb-10 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a href="#get-started" class="btn btn-primary">Get started fast</a>
        <a href="#permanent" class="btn btn-secondary"
          >Make it permanent later</a
        >
      </nav>

      <figure class="border-line bg-well mb-12 rounded-2xl border p-5 sm:p-8">
        <ol
          class="grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-3"
          aria-label="Connect the antenna to the USB receiver, then the receiver to your computer"
        >
          <li class="flex items-center gap-4 sm:flex-col sm:items-start">
            <svg
              viewBox="0 0 100 100"
              class="text-accent size-20 shrink-0"
              fill="none"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="3"
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
              <path d="M35 5v36m0 18v36"></path>
              <rect x="27" y="41" width="16" height="18" rx="3"></rect>
              <path d="M43 50h20q15 0 15 15v15h17"></path>
            </svg>
            <div>
              <p class="font-semibold">1. Antenna</p><p
                class="text-fg-muted text-sm"
              >
                By a window facing the water
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-center gap-4 sm:flex-col sm:items-start">
            <svg
              viewBox="0 0 100 100"
              class="text-accent size-20 shrink-0"
              fill="none"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="3"
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
              <path d="M4 50h18m55 0h18"></path>
              <rect x="22" y="32" width="55" height="36" rx="5"></rect>
              <path d="M27 44h13m-13 12h26m29-17h12v22H82"></path>
            </svg>
            <div>
              <p class="font-semibold">2. USB receiver</p><p
                class="text-fg-muted text-sm"
              >
                Antenna cable screws into it
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-center gap-4 sm:flex-col sm:items-start">
            <svg
              viewBox="0 0 100 100"
              class="text-accent size-20 shrink-0"
              fill="none"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="3"
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
              <rect x="15" y="15" width="70" height="50" rx="4"></rect>
              <path d="m15 65-9 15h88L85 65M29 49l12-10 13 5 19-17"></path>
            </svg>
            <div>
              <p class="font-semibold">3. Your computer</p><p
                class="text-fg-muted text-sm"
              >
                Receiver plugs into USB
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <figcaption
          class="text-fg-muted border-line mt-6 border-t pt-4 text-sm"
        >
          The antenna hears ships. AIS-catcher software turns those signals into
          a local map. Your internet connection shares the receptions with Open
          Waters.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <section id="get-started" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="mb-4">Get started fast</h2>
        <p class="text-fg-muted mb-8">
          Before buying, check your spot: can you put an antenna near a window
          overlooking a harbor, anchorage, or shipping lane? Hills and buildings
          can block radio signals. A ship on the Open Waters map may be heard by
          another station; it doesn't guarantee you'll hear it from home.
        </p>

        <Card class="mb-12">
          <h3 class="mb-3 text-2xl">Buy this antenna and receiver kit</h3>
          <p class="mb-3 font-semibold">
            RTL-SDR Blog V3 with Dipole Antenna Kit
          </p>
          <p class="text-fg-muted mb-4">
            Choose the USB-A V3 <em>with the antenna kit</em>, rather than the
            dongle-only option. An SDR is a radio receiver that uses your
            computer to decode the signals.
          </p>
          <ul class="text-fg-muted mb-5 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
            <li>
              USB receiver and a matching antenna with two long telescopic
              elements
            </li>
            <li>
              Antenna cable, 3 m extension cable, window mount, and small tripod
            </li>
            <li>
              Only extra: a USB-C to USB-A data adapter if your computer has no
              USB-A port
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="mb-5 text-sm">
            Kit price: US$44.95–49.95 on the manufacturer's store, checked
            October 11, 2026. Tax, delivery, and any USB adapter are extra;
            local prices vary.
          </p>
          <a href={STORE} class="btn btn-primary">Find the V3 antenna kit</a>
          <p class="text-fg-muted mt-4 text-sm">
            The store lists its official regional sellers. The kit's antenna
            connector already fits the receiver. Use your existing Windows, Mac,
            or Linux computer and internet connection.
          </p>
        </Card>

        <section id="connect" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h3 class="mb-4">1. Connect the antenna and receiver</h3>
          <ol class="text-fg-muted list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6">
            <li>
              Screw the two longer telescopic elements into the antenna base.
              Extend each to about 42 cm of exposed length.
            </li>
            <li>
              Mount the antenna indoors at your best window, with one element
              pointing up and the other down.
            </li>
            <li>
              Screw the antenna cable's small metal connector into the receiver.
              Use the included extension if needed to reach the window.
            </li>
            <li>
              Plug the receiver into your computer's USB port, using a data
              adapter if needed.
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p class="text-fg-muted mt-4 text-sm">
            That's all the hardware for this setup. The <a
              href="https://www.rtl-sdr.com/using-our-new-dipole-antenna-kit/"
              >manufacturer's illustrated antenna guide</a
            >
            shows the mounts and element lengths. Keep this antenna indoors for ongoing
            use.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="install" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h3 class="mb-4">2. Install AIS-catcher on your computer</h3>
          <p class="text-fg-muted mb-5">
            AIS-catcher is free software that receives ships and gives you a
            dashboard in your browser. Follow the instructions for your
            computer.
          </p>
          <nav
            aria-label="Choose your computer"
            class="mb-6 flex flex-wrap gap-4"
          >
            <a href="#windows">Windows</a><a href="#mac">Mac</a><a href="#linux"
              >Linux</a
            >
          </nav>
          <details
            id="windows"
            class="border-line mb-4 scroll-mt-24 rounded-xl border p-5"
            open
          >
            <summary class="cursor-pointer text-lg font-semibold"
              >Windows</summary
            >
            <ol class="text-fg-muted mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6">
              <li>
                Download the <a
                  href={`${DOCS}/installation/windows/#recent-releases`}
                  >AIS-catcher Edge x64 ZIP</a
                > for a typical Intel/AMD Windows PC and extract it into a folder.
                Windows on ARM needs a separate compatibility check.
              </li>
              <li>
                Download and open <a href="https://zadig.akeo.ie/">Zadig</a>. In
                Options, select <strong>List All Devices</strong>. Select the
                RTL-SDR's <strong>Bulk-In, Interface (Interface 0)</strong> device,
                choose <strong>WinUSB</strong>, and click Install Driver or
                Replace Driver. Confirm you selected the dongle, not another USB
                device. This is a one-time step.
              </li>
              <li>
                In the extracted AIS-catcher folder, double-click <code
                  >start-GUI.bat</code
                >. Leave its window open while receiving.
              </li>
            </ol>
            <p class="text-fg-muted mt-4 text-sm">
              If it won't launch, the <a href={`${DOCS}/installation/windows/`}
                >Windows guide</a
              > links the Microsoft runtime it needs.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details
            id="mac"
            class="border-line mb-4 scroll-mt-24 rounded-xl border p-5"
          >
            <summary class="cursor-pointer text-lg font-semibold">Mac</summary>
            <p class="text-fg-muted mt-4 mb-4">
              The Mac setup uses Terminal and builds the software. Open Terminal
              from Applications → Utilities. If you don't have Homebrew, follow
              the installation instructions at <a href="https://brew.sh/"
                >brew.sh</a
              >, including its final shell setup steps. Accept Apple's
              command-line tools installation if prompted.
            </p>
            <p class="text-fg-muted mb-3">
              Run these commands in Terminal, one line at a time. The build may
              take several minutes.
            </p>
            <CodeBlock code={installMac} class="mb-4" />
            <p class="text-fg-muted mb-3">
              The last command asks for your Mac login password; Terminal
              doesn't show it as you type. Then start the dashboard:
            </p>
            <CodeBlock code="AIS-catcher -E ~/aiscatcher.json 127.0.0.1:8118" />
            <p class="text-fg-muted mt-4 text-sm">
              Leave Terminal running while receiving. If a command fails, use
              the <a href={`${DOCS}/installation/macos/`}
                >Mac installation guide</a
              >.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details
            id="linux"
            class="border-line scroll-mt-24 rounded-xl border p-5"
          >
            <summary class="cursor-pointer text-lg font-semibold">Linux</summary
            >
            <p class="text-fg-muted mt-4 mb-3">
              On Ubuntu or Debian, open Terminal and run the official installer
              below. It downloads AIS-catcher, installs its dependencies, and
              sets it up as a background service. Enter your administrator
              password if asked.
            </p>
            <CodeBlock code={installLinux} class="mb-4" />
            <p class="text-fg-muted text-sm">
              If curl is missing, run <code>sudo apt install curl</code> first. For
              another distribution, follow the <a
                href={`${DOCS}/installation/overview/`}
                >platform installation guide</a
              >.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <section id="first-ships" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h3 class="mb-4">3. See the ships your antenna hears</h3>
          <ol class="text-fg-muted list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6">
            <li>
              On the same computer, open <a href="http://localhost:8118/"
                >http://localhost:8118</a
              > in your browser. This is your receiver's dashboard.
            </li>
            <li>
              If asked, set a dashboard admin password. Follow the setup wizard
              and select your RTL-SDR USB receiver as the input.
            </li>
            <li>
              Skip the optional sharing outputs for now. Choose <strong
                >Save &amp; Close</strong
              > with <strong>Start the receiver after saving</strong> unchecked.
            </li>
            <li>
              Under <strong>Output → Community</strong>, turn{" "}
              <strong>Enable Sharing</strong> off. Skipping the wizard's community
              step alone leaves sharing enabled. Save, then press{" "}
              <strong>Start</strong>. You can choose to share later.
            </li>
            <li>
              Look at the dashboard's local map and received-message count. With
              traffic in range, the count increases and ships appear.
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p class="text-fg-muted mt-4">
            You're receiving ships directly from your window. Give the receiver
            time to hear passing traffic; some ship details arrive less often
            than positions. If nothing arrives, <a href="#help"
              >check these fixes</a
            >
            before buying more equipment.
          </p>
          <p class="text-fg-muted mt-3 text-sm">
            Keep your computer awake while receiving. You can close the browser
            tab; AIS-catcher itself must stay running. Keep the dashboard on
            your home network and don't forward its port through your router.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="share" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h3 class="mb-4">4. Share your receptions with Open Waters</h3>
          <p class="text-fg-muted mb-4">
            Once you can see local ships, add Open Waters as an output in
            AIS-catcher. You'll use a private sharing key, called a token. No
            account or router changes are needed.
          </p>
          <ol class="text-fg-muted mb-5 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6">
            <li>
              Open <a
                href="/ais/token/"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer">the token page in a new tab</a
              >. Optionally give your station a public name, accept the
              contributor agreement, and generate your token. Leave the
              network-address binding option unchecked.
            </li>
            <li>
              Copy your token. In the receiver dashboard, open <strong
                >Output</strong
              >, select <strong>MQTT</strong>, and add an output.
            </li>
            <li>
              Set the fields below. In the URL, replace <code>YOUR_TOKEN</code> with
              the token you copied. Expand <strong>Advanced settings</strong>,
              then find <strong>Message Format</strong> under{" "}
              <strong>Format</strong> and choose <strong>NMEA</strong>. Leave
              the other settings at their defaults.
            </li>
          </ol>
          <CodeBlock code={sharingUrl} class="mb-4" />
          <dl class="mb-5">
            <dt>URL</dt><dd class="min-w-0">
              The full address above, with your token
            </dd>
            <dt>Message Format</dt><dd>NMEA</dd>
            <dt>Active</dt><dd>On</dd>
            <dt>Topic</dt><dd><code>ais/data</code> (the default)</dd>
          </dl>
          <p class="text-fg-muted mb-4">
            Press <strong>Save</strong>, then restart the receiver in the
            dashboard to apply the output. This sends the ship messages your
            antenna hears over an encrypted connection.
          </p>
          <p class="text-fg-muted text-sm">
            Treat your token like a password; don't post it or include it in
            screenshots. Your station name and shared receptions are public. The <a
              href="https://github.com/openwatersio/aiscast/blob/main/docs/contributor-agreement.md"
              >contributor agreement</a
            >
            dedicates your receptions to the public domain (CC0), and the aggregate
            database is published under ODbL. AIS-catcher's separate community feed
            is optional; review it under Output → Community.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="confirm" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h3 class="mb-4">5. Open your station page</h3>
          <p class="text-fg-muted mb-4">
            Return to the token tab and follow your station's link. Check that
            it shows recent receptions while your local receiver is hearing
            ships. That confirms your data is reaching Open Waters; finding the
            same ship on the worldwide map alone doesn't.
          </p>
          <Card>
            <p class="mb-2 font-semibold">You're contributing.</p>
            <p class="text-fg-muted">
              Keep using this setup whenever your computer is on. To stop
              sharing, turn off this output's Active setting, save, and restart
              the receiver. You can keep receiving locally.
            </p>
          </Card>
        </section>

        <section id="help" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
          <h3 class="mb-5">If no ships appear</h3>
          <div class="space-y-5">
            {
              troubleshooting.map(({ symptom, action }) => (
                <div>
                  <>
                    <h4 class="mb-1 text-base">{symptom}</h4>
                    <p class="text-fg-muted">{action}</p>
                  </>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </div>
          <p class="text-fg-muted mt-5 text-sm">
            Still stuck? <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>Email us</a> with your
            computer type and the step you're on, without your token. See also <a
              href={`${DOCS}/advanced/troubleshooting/`}
              >AIS-catcher troubleshooting</a
            >.
          </p>
        </section>
      </section>

      <section id="permanent" class="border-line scroll-mt-24 border-t pt-10">
        <p class="text-fg-muted mb-3 text-sm">Optional next step</p>
        <h2 class="mb-4">Make it permanent</h2>
        <p class="text-fg-muted mb-8">
          Start here once your computer setup works. Reuse your receiver and
          Open Waters token. A dedicated computer can keep it running; an
          outdoor antenna can improve the view of the water. You can do either
          separately, and keep the starter setup for as long as it suits you.
        </p>
        <details class="border-line mb-6 rounded-xl border p-5">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer text-lg font-semibold"
            >Leave a Raspberry Pi running</summary
          >
          <p class="text-fg-muted mt-4 mb-4">
            A Pi is a small computer you can leave beside the antenna. It uses
            the same USB receiver and indoor antenna. Budget for a computer,
            power supply, case and storage in addition to your starter kit;
            you'll also prepare its software and home-network access.
          </p>
          <ul class="text-fg-muted mb-5 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
            <li>
              <a
                href="https://www.raspberrypi.com/products/raspberry-pi-4-model-b/"
                >Raspberry Pi 4 Model B, 2 GB</a
              >, or a compatible Pi you already own
            </li>
            <li>
              <a
                href="https://www.raspberrypi.com/products/type-c-power-supply/"
                >Official 15 W USB-C power supply</a
              > with your region's plug
            </li>
            <li>
              <a
                href="https://www.raspberrypi.com/products/raspberry-pi-4-case/"
                >Pi 4 case</a
              >
            </li>
            <li>
              32 GB microSD card and a card reader for your setup computer, if
              it doesn't have one
            </li>
            <li>
              Your home Wi-Fi, or an Ethernet cable for a wired connection
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-fg-muted mb-4 text-sm">
            Check bundle contents before buying. Pi prices vary by region; these
            purchases are extra to the antenna kit. No new antenna is required.
          </p>
          <ol class="text-fg-muted mb-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6">
            <li>
              On your existing computer, install <a
                href="https://www.raspberrypi.com/software/"
                >Raspberry Pi Imager</a
              >. Select Pi 4 and Raspberry Pi OS Lite (64-bit), then your
              microSD card. Writing the image erases the selected card, so
              double-check the device.
            </li>
            <li>
              In Imager's customization, set hostname <code>aisreceiver</code>,
              your own username and password, Wi-Fi details and country, and
              enable SSH with password authentication. Follow the <a
                href="https://www.raspberrypi.com/documentation/computers/getting-started.html"
                >official setup guide</a
              > if the screen labels differ.
            </li>
            <li>
              Eject the card, put it in the Pi, move the USB receiver from your
              computer to the Pi, and connect power. Stop the old receiver
              software first.
            </li>
            <li>
              From Terminal on a Mac/Linux computer or PowerShell on Windows,
              run <code>ssh YOUR_USERNAME@aisreceiver.local</code>, replacing <code
                >YOUR_USERNAME</code
              >. Confirm the connection to your Pi, then enter the password you
              set. If the hostname isn't found, use the Pi's local IP address
              from your router.
            </li>
            <li>In that SSH session, run the installer below.</li>
          </ol>
          <CodeBlock code={installLinux} class="mb-4" />
          <p class="text-fg-muted mb-4">
            From your normal computer, open <a
              href="http://aisreceiver.local:8118/"
              >http://aisreceiver.local:8118</a
            >. Follow <a href="#first-ships">the local reception steps</a>, then
            repeat <a href="#share">the sharing step</a> using your existing token.
            Don't generate a new token on the Pi; reusing it keeps your station identity.
          </p>
          <p class="text-fg-muted">
            The installer sets up a background service. In the SSH session, run <code
              >sudo reboot</code
            >; the connection will close. After the Pi starts again, check both
            its local dashboard and your Open Waters station page to confirm
            receiving and sharing resume. Leave its dashboard on your home
            network.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details class="border-line mb-8 rounded-xl border p-5">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer text-lg font-semibold"
            >Install a permanent outdoor antenna</summary
          >
          <p class="text-fg-muted mt-4 mb-4">
            If buildings or a low window limit reception, an outdoor location
            with a clearer view may help. This adds the cost and work of a
            weatherproof marine VHF/AIS antenna, mount, cable, matching
            connectors and an installation suitable for your site. Your computer
            or Pi can keep using the same SDR.
          </p>
          <p class="text-fg-muted">
            The kit's telescopic antenna isn't made for permanent outdoor use.
            Before purchasing, follow the <a href={`${DOCS}/what-you-need/`}
              >AIS-catcher equipment guidance</a
            >
            and your antenna manufacturer's mounting and grounding instructions. This
            is an advanced installation, optional for contributing.
          </p>
        </details>
        <p class="text-fg-muted text-sm">
          Already have a dAISy? It decodes AIS itself, but still needs a
          computer to share its messages. Use the <a
            href="https://wegmatt.com/docs.html"
            >dedicated receiver instructions</a
          >. Equipment and software links checked October 11, 2026. The setup
          instructions follow the manufacturers' documentation; these hardware
          combinations have not been tested by Open Waters.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </Container>
</MainLayout>
